[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem of ZFC (counterexample via Easton's theorem), and its negation is also [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnot a theorem (GCH provides a model where it holds).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write this up cleanly.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I should also check: the problem says "infinite cardinals." Both $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\aleph_0$ and $\aleph_1$ are infinite. Good.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also verify the Easton's theorem application more carefully. Easton's [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem applies to regular cardinals. $\aleph_0$ and $\aleph_1$ are both [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mregular. The function I defined needs to be consistent on all regular cardinals,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mnot just these two. I defined $F(\kappa) = \kappa^+$ for all regular $\kappa [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\geq \aleph_2$, and $F(\aleph_0) = F(\aleph_1) = \aleph_2$. This is a valid [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mEaston function.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, wait. I need to be a bit more careful. Easton's theorem requires that [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$F$ is defined on all regular cardinals and is non-decreasing and satisfies [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mKönig's theorem. Let me re-examine.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor regular cardinals $\kappa$, in increasing order: $\aleph_0, \aleph_1, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\aleph_2, \aleph_3, \ldots, \aleph_\omega, \ldots$ (well, $\aleph_\omega$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msingular, so skip), and so on.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$F(\aleph_0) = \aleph_2$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$F(\aleph_1) = \aleph_2$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$F(\aleph_2) = \aleph_3$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$F(\aleph_3) = \aleph_4$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m...[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNon-decreasing: $\aleph_2 \leq \aleph_2 \leq \aleph_3 \leq \aleph_4 \leq \ldots$[0m[38;2;124;124;124m✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKönig: $\text{cf}(\aleph_2) = \aleph_2 > \aleph_1 \geq \aleph_0$ ✓, and $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{cf}(\aleph_{n+1}) = \aleph_{n+1} > \aleph_n$ for $n \geq 2$ ✓.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor larger regular cardinals, $F(\kappa) = \kappa^+$, $\text{cf}(\kappa^+) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\kappa^+ > \kappa$ ✓.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo this is a valid Easton function, and Easton's theorem gives us a model where [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthis is the continuum function on regular cardinals. For singular cardinals, the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcontinuum function is determined by the regular cardinals (via Silver's theorem [0m[38;2;124;124;124mand other constraints, but in this case it's fine).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the proof is complete.[0m

